WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.280 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  thank you thank you very much thank you  all for being here Thank You Patricia  for organizing this it is true indeed  the idea of having this event in the  occasion of my visit here in Japan the  second in a month in the occasion of the  g20 foreign ministers meeting came out  of a conversation we had just less than  one month ago and we thought that the  excellent bilateral relationship we have  between the European Union and Japan  could have also been deepened and  showcased on this very important topic  of the role of women and young people in  the peace and security work globally  because we are partners on trade with  partners on political issues between the  European Union and Japan but we're also  partners in society and were also  partners in the global work we do to  advance a peace and security agenda that  can be inclusive and probably  sustainable involving fully young people  and and women I would like to thank you  all for being here for making this  possible because we are not just to  discuss a specific policy or program  today although of course we also need  the right policies and programs and this  is why I'm so honored and happy to have  with us today some of the key  decision-makers here in Japan members of  parliament and Minister corner that is  joining us in a few minutes but we are  here to discuss a global change of  mindset embodied by two UN Security  Council resolutions that are at the  heart of the foreign and security policy  for sure of the European Union and I  believe also of Japan a pretty radical  change of mindset the international  community finally accepted collectively  that women and youth are not just the  object of our policies the recipients of  decisions that are taken by others and  these others are often men and often  over 60 years old women and young people  are agents of change  in their lives in their friendships and  the relationships in their families in  their communities in the countries and  also globally if we don't recognize this  if we don't acknowledge this fact we  condemn our policies and our actions to  being ineffective it is as simple as  that we are here first and foremost  because we recognize a fact a reality in  our societies in our countries in the  21st century you have all seen some  images that have I believe inspired us  all you've seen the images of a Sudanese  young woman standing on the top of a car  asking for democracy in our country the  colder the Sudanese Statute of Liberty  and I just met the new prime minister of  Sudan a few weeks ago in Brussels in  hornaday's with a visit and that change  came about and started from there  from participation from active  participation of a young woman we all  know about the leading role of our sons  and daughters in the global movement for  climate action without them that key  policy issue would probably not be at  the forefront of the political landscape  globally across the world young people  are the majority in many of the  societies and of course women are half  of the global population in these years  I've had the honour of meeting many of  those people there are the leading force  behind women peace and security and  youth peace and security I will only  mention the former Colombian children  soldiers who finally had access to  education when the war in the country  ended the Syrian and Yemeni women were  working with the United Nations with the  support of the European Union to try to  build peace and reconciliation in the  countries because there is no real peace  and security without them peace deals  are usually signed by two men the two  leaders of the two sides at war this  might begin  with that signature that is a key  starting point but that signature is  never enough to build peace on the  ground for real peace can only grow and  stand the test of time  if it has deep roots in the societies  that have been in conflict and for this  you need youth and women without them  there's no peace you need the silent  majority and you need the silent  majority to become less silent to engage  because this majority was not just a  victim of war or a hostage rather of war  this majority can be the agents of  change and moving from war conflicts  tensions to peace requires deep changes  the most powerful agent of peace and  reconciliation in societies are always  women and young people at the same is  true to prevent conflicts before they  arise a country will be much less stable  if it's young people cannot find a  decent job or cannot express their ideas  freely if they don't find their place in  society I have always been impressed by  my dialogue for instance with young  generation in some of the Arab countries  and when I asked them why do you think  your people your age your generation  sometimes are attracted by becoming a  foreign fighter in another country or  attracted by the - ideology they often  told me it's not just a matter of  economy or having a job it's a matter of  having a place in the organic gram of  society of being part of something  belonging somewhere and I think this  element of inclusion of giving a role to  play to each and every one of us is a  key component also of preventing  violence and conflict in the same way  violence against women includes many  different forms from economic injustice  to social disparities lack of fairer  healthcare as well as physical mental  emotional  violence against mainly women and girls  and when a society is not just there can  be no sustainable peace either there are  policies that we can put in place to  address this imbalances policies to give  our young people and women the  opportunities that they serve and also  that our societies need or to involve  women and youth in a peace process but  beyond changing policies which is  essential we need a change of attitudes  which implies also an individual effort  we need to teach our boys and girls that  violence is always wrong that violence  is not a demonstration of strength but  rather the opposite is always a  demonstration of weakness and those that  take violent attitudes especially  against women and girls are actually  showing their weakness it's simply not  cool I think this should be the message  that our young boys in particular should  reflect in their behaviors and their our  relations with their peers and we need  to stop thinking that by opening our  decision-making to youth and women we're  doing them a favor I'm convinced that  we're doing a favor to all of us and in  particular we're doing a favor to our  democracies because if a democracy is  not able to reflect the composition of  the population if the voters are not  able to identify themselves in those  that are being elected that is a  weakness for our democratic systems this  is the only way to make decision-making  really inclusive and also sustainable I  believe and it's also the only way to  really give peace a chance so we will  continue to work with Japan with all of  you to change our collective attitudes  that start with our individual behaviors  and to do it together and to recognize  youth and women as true fundamental  agents of change to support them not  just because they need it but because  the entire society needs that I thank  you very much  [Applause]

